$[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=8{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=8{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9da8aaa1-371e-542a-b735-cc14917f27c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365271} {:answer/id #uuid "2a160f3e-a855-5243-a0e9-c5f03bf8f679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365272} {:answer/id #uuid "fb380057-e757-5bd8-99d9-76a4a7ee9167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365273} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c1bed-5856-5e79-96d4-616f650287ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274} {:answer/id #uuid "20858cfc-ee8e-5871-9334-6cd0baf766a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb380057-e757-5bd8-99d9-76a4a7ee9167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365273} :answer-field/id #uuid "76171955-2021-5f42-9e85-24b9adf9d623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "217d7c6a-01fa-5c09-8fc9-462942a415a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38884" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1649d14f-54d9-5949-a983-9ee90b1747db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365278} {:answer/id #uuid "c04fc26a-43fb-5262-bcfa-6cb2b3a6bb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{6} & 2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365279} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bd5202-7edb-59e5-8a10-76cb9fb6bcab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280} {:answer/id #uuid "0d61ca26-0a90-54af-9db0-7dd60cd4a9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365281} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7ca8b1-7d89-514b-a1c7-2184b24900f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7ca8b1-7d89-514b-a1c7-2184b24900f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365282} :answer-field/id #uuid "90fbf554-932a-5d03-80b4-8ca780d58d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2244cd7a-51c5-5d7a-adfd-69f19a073f69" :question/math-academy-id "q-38918" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 3 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbb0c906-41aa-5fbd-a9ef-2ed1b9374135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365285} {:answer/id #uuid "e1afe158-a3bd-5472-acb5-5d8f2311e133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365286} {:answer/id #uuid "45cf231b-bcb0-550b-a16b-e1a5eac1516b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365287} {:answer/id #uuid "59707974-1c8a-5bcd-9699-b38eb1633877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288} {:answer/id #uuid "f780fbd2-e3b7-5817-b790-2fd8ae2810ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45cf231b-bcb0-550b-a16b-e1a5eac1516b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365287} :answer-field/id #uuid "3703df40-3179-5bd7-ac77-0777e13c696f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9e52e1a-2cfc-5e9d-954d-1a0bc9675bab" :question/math-academy-id "q-82474" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Three-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06efe20-a1ee-526e-8cc1-6f434ef554ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365292} {:answer/id #uuid "ead2ede2-4ea0-5dc0-bca9-f1da1d16940d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365293} {:answer/id #uuid "369aeef7-3872-5f7c-bd40-d2abc45540fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365294} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bef8eb-cc2c-5a71-af62-a2025f9cca8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365295} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd6f76e-b089-53e4-8795-33b7dfecdcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9bef8eb-cc2c-5a71-af62-a2025f9cca8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365295} :answer-field/id #uuid "631d070c-197a-547c-89c3-d64b47b42139" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90b6c4fc-18d0-517d-977f-8977fe2f7ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82496" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & c \\\\ a & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & b & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x-y+z \\\\ x+y \\\\ y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+2=4.\n$$"}]}
